Offsets: a B-spline face is enlarged at C2 across a side that is the
iso-line of a tangential edge (mitcad#1202).

The case: a loft from a square to a circle of radius 20
(BRepOffsetAPI_ThruSections) whose circle's seam falls on a side, offset
3 mm inwards closed. The loft splits that side into two faces tangent
along the edge between them. BRepOffset_Inter3d enlarges each half's
offset for the intersections (BRepOffset_Tool::EnLargeFace,
GeomLib::ExtendSurfByLength at C1), also across that edge. The offset of
a surface extended at C1 has a crease along the junction, and the curves
where the circle's end cuts the halves' offsets end on the tangential
edge's offset just there: their approximations, running across the
crease, came out up to 1.06e-3 mm off their faces and their vertex at
that end (the edges' tolerances raised to cover it), and the volume
1.6e-5 of it off that of the same loft with the seam on a corner.

BRepOffset_Inter3d now extends a B-spline or Bezier surface at C2 before
its first or after its last U where that side is the iso-line of an edge
the analysis finds tangential all along (the new
ContinuityAcrossTangentialSides; EnLargeFace and EnlargeGeometry get the
continuity of each U side as parameters, 1 by default). Every other side
and surface is extended as before. The edges are then within 2.1e-4 mm
and the volume that of the seam on a corner within 3e-8.


That changes the extended surfaces, and with them where the intersections
at the corners of an octagon to a circle of radius 21.7 (seam on a side,
3 mm) meet: there the curves on the circle's end meet nearly tangentially
at a corner whose crease flattens to tangency, and the vertex
BRepOffset_MakeLoops substitutes keeps points on its edges up to
1.4e-4 mm off it. Patch 1190's CoverPoints widens that vertex's tolerance
to them, as for the octagon to a circle of radius 24.5 at 1 and 2 mm.
And where the walls lean in (a square to a circle of radius 10, its seam
on a side, shelled 1 mm), the intersection at the corner of the extended
half left a gap about the tangency of 8 % of the offset on Windows;
patch 1190 takes the continuation from within 20 % there (mitcad#1217).
